# Documentation Governance

Keep project documentation useful for humans and AI agents by separating intent, decisions, implementation knowledge, and evolution history.

## Document Types

- `docs/intent/`: WHAT the product or feature does and WHY it matters. Do not include implementation details.
- `docs/decisions/`: HOW a non-trivial technical decision was made, including rationale and alternatives.
- `docs/knowledge/patterns/`: HOW TO IMPLEMENT reusable patterns.
- `docs/knowledge/anti-patterns/`: implementation shapes to avoid.
- `docs/knowledge/guides/`: step-by-step guides.
- `docs/knowledge/reference/`: broad reference material, diagrams, and glossaries.
- `docs/evolution/`: relevant changes, outcomes, and learnings.

## Rules

- Create an ADR before implementing non-trivial architecture, integration, persistence, security, or API contract decisions.
- Use one canonical source per topic; other files should link to it instead of duplicating content.
- Add a `Status` section to durable docs: `Created`, `Status`, and `Note`.
- Link related intent, decisions, patterns, anti-patterns, and implementation files.
- Update context docs and changelog when functionality or standards change.

## Do Not

- Put technical implementation details in intent files.
- Turn root `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, or rules into encyclopedias.
- Duplicate pattern explanations across multiple files.
- Create docs for trivial changes where no reusable knowledge or decision exists.
